# Geppetto build target: dgx-spark
#
# aarch64 + NVIDIA GB10 Grace Blackwell (sm_121): DGX Spark and equivalents.
#
# STATUS: UNVERIFIED. Written against documented GB10 requirements; nothing here
# has been built or run on the hardware yet. Three known risks, in order:
#
#  1. sm_121 kernels. Stock PyTorch wheels compile only to sm_120, leaving GB10
#     on PTX JIT. Simple ops work; diffusion sampling — which is exactly what
#     TRELLIS.2 does — has been reported to hang indefinitely in JIT. If that
#     happens, point TORCH_INDEX_URL at an sm_121-native build.
#  2. Sparse attention has no soft fallback. trellis2/modules/sparse/config.py
#     accepts only xformers / flash_attn / flash_attn_3 — no sdpa, no naive. No
#     flash-attn wheel exists for aarch64+sm_121 and FA2 targets sm_80/sm_90,
#     so this target builds xformers from source. If xformers will not build
#     for sm_121, this target does not run, full stop.
#  3. The six CUDA extensions (nvdiffrast, nvdiffrec, cumesh, flexgemm,
#     o-voxel) are all source builds against CUDA 13 on arm64. Each is a
#     separate layer, so failures are isolated and legible.
#
# Expect it to be slower than the nvidia-cuda target regardless: GB10's unified
# memory runs ~273 GB/s against an RTX 6000 Ada's ~960 GB/s, and this workload
# is bandwidth-bound. The Spark's advantage is being a dedicated always-on box
# with 128 GB, not raw speed. VRAM is a non-issue — peak measured is 11.3 GB.
BASE_IMAGE=nvidia/cuda:13.0.0-devel-ubuntu24.04
CUDA_ARCH=12.1
PYTHON_VERSION=3.12
TORCH_SPEC=torch torchvision
TORCH_INDEX_URL=https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu130
ATTN_PACKAGE=xformers
ATTN_PACKAGE_FALLBACK=xformers
ATTN_IMPORT=xformers
DEFAULT_ATTN_BACKEND=xformers
GEPPETTO_TARGET=dgx-spark
